{
  "abstract": "Let D_p(n) count zeros minus ones in the canonical base-p expansion, for any odd integer p at least three. We determine its joint local distribution along every fixed finite trajectory of the maps n/2 on even inputs and (pn+e)/2 on odd inputs, for either sign e. Each prescribed parity word has a Gaussian counting law uniform over integer balance vectors, with explicit positive-definite covariance. Parity words are asymptotically uniform conditional on a balance vector in a fixed central window. Every finite parity word occurs on arbitrarily large balanced trajectories, although fixed-time survival proportions decay. We prove that every eventually invariant set eventually described by this counter and a fixed dyadic residue is finite or cofinite; a quantitative escape bound treats arbitrary acceptance rules. Explicit short-loop certificates also bound prescribed balance vectors by X exp(-(c+o(1))(log log X)^(3/2)) through time floor(sqrt(log log X/(3 log 2))), where c=1/(3 sqrt(3 log 2)). For ordinary 3x+1, a separate constructive proof shows that eventual invariance and arbitrary dependence on the full ternary digit histogram and a fixed dyadic residue again force finite or cofinite membership. The proofs use an integer cycle lattice, canonical trajectory reductions, Fourier analysis, and exact histogram transitions. Finite checks and scoped independent audits support the constructions. The histogram theorem has no independent audit yet. None of the results resolves Collatz.",
